Subject: sysutils/augeas: fix NULL dereference (CVE-2025-2588)
From: zakinko@snowrabbit.org
Reply-To: zakinko@snowrabbit.org
X-send-pr-version: 3.95

>Submitter-Id:	net
>Originator:	Showta Ishizaki
>Organization:
>Confidential:	no
>Synopsis:	sysutils/augeas: fix NULL dereference (CVE-2025-2588)
>Severity:	non-critical
>Priority:	medium
>Category:	pkg
>Class:		sw-bug
>Release:	NetBSD 11.0, pkgsrc CVS as of 2026-08-14
>Environment:
System: NetBSD 11.0 i386
Architecture: i386
Machine: i386
>Description:
	pkg_admin audit reports CVE-2025-2588 against every version of
	sysutils/augeas, and there is no release to update to.

	parse_regexp() in src/fa.c can return NULL without recording a reason
	in parse->error.  Three of its callers -- fa_expand_nocase(),
	fa_restrict_alphabet() and fa_expand_char_ranges() -- only test
	parse->error before dereferencing what came back, so they walk into
	the NULL.  (A fourth caller, fa_compile(), tests the pointer and is
	fine.)

	Upstream fixed this by filling in parse->error at the error label
	rather than adding a NULL check to each caller, which closes all three
	at once:

	  https://github.com/hercules-team/augeas/issues/852
	  commit af2aa88ab37fc48167d8c5e43b1770a4ba2ff403, 2025-03-30

	That commit has never been released.  1.14.1 (2023-07-15) is still the
	last release, seventeen months after the fix landed, so waiting for
	upstream is not a plan.  Nor is moving to a snapshot worth it here:
	master is only 19 commits ahead of 1.14.1, and once the new Deb822
	apt sources lens and its tests are set aside, the C code differs by
	about 80 lines, nearly all of it -Wdiscarded-qualifiers cleanup for
	GCC 16.

	Other distributions already carry the fix.  Fedora ships it as a git
	snapshot (1.14.2-0.4.20250324git4dffa3d); Debian has it in bookworm,
	trixie and sid, naming the same commit; SUSE record it as resolved in
	bsc#1239909.  No BSD carries it yet -- FreeBSD textproc/augeas and
	OpenBSD sysutils/augeas both have patches, but none of them touch
	src/fa.c.

>How-To-Repeat:
	pkg_admin audit on a system with augeas installed reports

	  Package augeas-1.14.1nb1 has a denial-of-service vulnerability,
	  see https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-2588

	and the pkg-vulnerabilities entry is augeas-[0-9]*, so no version
	clears it.

	For the crash itself, apply the patch below with its src/fa.c hunk
	dropped, so that only the regression test goes in, then build and run
	that one test:

	  cd tests && make fatest && ./fatest

	It prints nothing and dies with SIGSEGV (exit 139).  With the whole
	patch applied it reports "OK (21 tests)".  (Observed on NetBSD
	9.4/i386; nothing in this path is OS specific.)

	"make test" shows it too, but augeas 1.14.1 already fails 7 of its 263
	tests without this patch, so fatest on its own is the clearer signal.
	See below.

>Fix:
	The patch is upstream commit af2aa88 applied verbatim -- the fix in
	src/fa.c, the fa.h comment that documents the new return value, and
	the regression test.  Nothing is adapted.

	It is one patch covering three files and named after the CVE rather
	than after a source file, following editors/emacs21 and net/avahi.
	Splitting it three ways would separate the fix from the test that
	demonstrates it.

	_REG_ENOSYS resolves even though NetBSD's <regex.h> does not have it,
	because augeas is built with -I ../gnulib/lib and its <regex.h> is the
	bundled gnulib copy, not the system header.  I checked this rather
	than assumed it; the preprocessor resolves <regex.h> from src/fa.c to
	../gnulib/lib/regex.h, which defines _REG_ENOSYS.

	PKGREVISION bumped.  Built and packaged on NetBSD 9.4, 10.1 and
	11.0/i386.

	fatest, which is where the CVE lives, passes on all three.  The rest of
	the suite does not, but that is not this patch: 1.14.1 fails these
	seven either way --

	  lens-sip_conf.sh   test-augprint.sh    test-idempotent.sh
	  lens-sudoers.sh    test-augtool.sh     test-interpreter.sh
	                     test-createfile.sh

	Reverting src/fa.c, src/fa.h and tests/fatest.c and running the suite
	again gives the same totals (263 run, 252 passed, 7 failed) and the
	same seven names.

	Please also narrow the pkg-vulnerabilities entry when this goes in.
	It currently reads

	  augeas-[0-9]*		denial-of-service	.../CVE-2025-2588

	and should become

	  augeas<1.14.1nb2	denial-of-service	.../CVE-2025-2588

	Without that, pkg_admin audit keeps reporting the CVE against the
	fixed package, because the entry matches every version.

